data: {"id":"chatcmpl-96af72ef-2912-4669-a078-d3378940e67c","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790493401,"model":"capitoline-chair-ladder","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":". However, the kernel does not declare the connection dead at dispatch; it must wait for the final `tcp_keepalive_intvl` (75 s) window to lapse without a response, yielding the final $7,875\\text{ s}$ mark.\n* **Timer Granularity:** In practice, keepalive timers are processed by the kernel's timer wheel and softirq infrastructure, so real-world expiration may exhibit minor jitter (typically a fraction of a second to a few seconds depending on `CONFIG_HZ` tick resolution and timer slack).\n* **Stock Defaults vs. Production Environments:** While $7,200\\text{ s}$, $75\\text{ s}$, and $9$ are the hardcoded upstream defaults in the Linux kernel source, many production Linux distributions, container runtimes, or cloud environments override these `sysctl` values via configuration files to detect dead peers significantly faster.\n"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-96af72ef-2912-4669-a078-d3378940e67c","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790493401,"model":"capitoline-chair-ladder","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":""},"finish_reason":null}],"capitoline":{"stage":"synthesis","done":1,"total":1}}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-96af72ef-2912-4669-a078-d3378940e67c","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790493401,"model":"capitoline-chair-ladder","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":99490,"completion_tokens":13096,"total_tokens":112586},"capitoline":{"provider":"capitoline","ignored":[],"council":{"deliberationId":"d2467723-3863-44d8-847f-61a28144538d","strategyVersion":2,"shape":"ranked","members":[{"family":"pro-high","model":"antigravity-gemini-pro-high","label":"Response B","answer":"On a stock, unmodified Linux kernel, the kernel will declare the connection dead after exactly **7,875 seconds** (which is 2 hours, 11 minutes, and 15 seconds).\n\nThis figure comes from three global `sysctl` parameters that govern TCP keepalives. On a stock kernel, their default values are:\n\n1. **`net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time` = 7200** (2 hours)\n   This is the amount of time the connection must remain completely idle (no data sent or received) before the kernel sends the very first TCP keepalive probe.\n2. **`net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_probes` = 9**\n   This is the maximum number of keepalive probes the kernel will send without receiving an acknowledgment (ACK) from the peer before deciding the connection is dead.\n3. **`net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_intvl` = 75** (75 seconds)\n   This is the time interval the kernel waits between sending each subsequent unacknowledged keepalive probe.\n\n**The calculation:**\nWhen the peer loses power silently and there is no application traffic:\n1. The socket sits completely idle for `tcp_keepalive_time` (7,200 seconds). \n2. At the 7,200-second mark, the kernel sends the 1st keepalive probe and resets its internal timer for `tcp_keepalive_intvl` (75 seconds).\n3. The peer is dead, so it never ACKs. The kernel sends a total of 9 probes, waiting 75 seconds after each one. \n4. After the 9th probe goes unacknowledged for its 75-second interval, the kernel hits the maximum probe limit and aborts the connection.\n\nTotal time = `tcp_keepalive_time` + (`tcp_keepalive_probes` × `tcp_keepalive_intvl`)\nTotal time = 7,200 + (9 × 75)\nTotal time = 7,200 + 675 = **7,875 seconds**\n\n**Caveat / Nuance:**\nWhile these are the hardcoded defaults in the upstream Linux kernel source code, it is worth noting that many modern Linux distributions, cloud providers, and container runtimes (like Docker) often modify these `sysctl` values by default to fail faster (e.g., dropping the 2-hour wait down to 5 minutes or less). However, strictly speaking of a \"stock\" default Linux configuration, the 7,875-second figure is the standard.\n"},{"family":"flash-high","model":"antigravity-gemini-flash-high","label":"Response A","answer":"### The Figure\n\nOn a stock Linux system, the kernel declares the connection dead after **7,875 seconds** (which is **2 hours, 11 minutes, and 15 seconds**).\n\n---\n\n### The Parameters\n\nThe timing is governed by three global `sysctl` kernel parameters under `/proc/sys/net/ipv4/`:\n\n1. **`net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time`** = **`7200`** seconds (2 hours)\n   * The idle time after the last received packet before the kernel transmits the first keepalive probe.\n2. **`net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_intvl`** = **`75`** seconds\n   * The interval between unacknowledged keepalive probes.\n3. **`net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_probes`** = **`9`**\n   * The number of unacknowledged keepalive probes sent before considering the connection dead.\n\n---\n\n### Timeline and Kernel Reasoning\n\nThe formula for the total time is:\n$$\\text{Total Time} = \\text{tcp\\_keepalive\\_time} + (\\text{tcp\\_keepalive\\_probes} \\times \\text{tcp\\_keepalive\\_intvl})$$\n$$7200 + (9 \\times 75) = 7200 + 675 = 7875\\text{ seconds}$$\n\nHere is how the Linux kernel (`net/ipv4/tcp_timer.c`) steps through the process:\n\n1. **$t = 0$ s**: The peer loses power and normal traffic ceases. The keepalive timer is armed for `tcp_keepalive_time` (7,200 s).\n2. **$t = 7,200$ s**: The idle timer fires. Probe counter (`icsk_probes_out`) is 0. Since $0 < 9$, the kernel sends **Probe #1** (an ACK segment with $SEG.SEQ = SND.NXT - 1$). The probe counter increments to 1, and the timer is re-armed for `tcp_keepalive_intvl` (75 s).\n3. **$t = 7,275$ s**: Timer fires. Probe counter is 1 ($1 < 9$). **Probe #2** is sent; counter becomes 2. Timer re-armed for 75 s.\n4. *(probes 3 through 8 follow every 75 seconds)*\n5. **$t = 7,800$ s** ($7200 + 8 \\times 75$): Timer fires. Probe counter is 8 ($8 < 9$). **Probe #9** (the final probe) is sent; counter becomes 9. The timer is re-armed for 75 s.\n6. **$t = 7,875$ s** ($7200 + 9 \\times 75$): Timer fires. The kernel checks `icsk_probes_out >= tcp_keepalive_probes` ($9 \\ge 9$). Because the 9th probe also went unacknowledged for the duration of `tcp_keepalive_intvl`, the kernel does not send another probe. It tears down the connection, flags the socket with `ETIMEDOUT`, and wakes any waiting threads with an error.\n\n*(Note: If a calculation measures up to the moment the **last probe is dispatched**, that occurs at 7,800 seconds; however, the kernel does not declare the connection dead until probe #9 times out at 7,875 seconds.)*\n"},{"family":"flash-low","model":"antigravity-gemini-flash-low","label":"Response C","answer":"On a stock Linux system, the kernel will declare the connection dead after **approximately 7,425 seconds** (or **2 hours, 3 minutes, and 45 seconds**).\n\n---\n\n### The Parameters and Default Values\n\nThe calculation comes from three system-wide kernel sysctl parameters (located in `/proc/sys/net/ipv4/`):\n\n1. **`tcp_keepalive_time` = `7200` seconds (2 hours)**\n   * The duration the connection must remain completely idle before the kernel transmits the very first keepalive probe.\n2. **`tcp_keepalive_intvl` = `75` seconds**\n   * The interval between successive keepalive probe retransmissions if no ACK is received.\n3. **`tcp_keepalive_probes` = `9` probes**\n   * The maximum number of unanswered keepalive probes sent before giving up and closing the connection.\n\n---\n\n### The Timing Breakdown\n\n1. **Idle Phase:** The connection is idle for **7,200 seconds**.\n2. **Probe Phase:**\n   * Probe 1 is sent at $t = 7200\\text{ s}$.\n   * Probes 2 through 9 are sent every 75 seconds after Probe 1:\n     $$\\text{Probe 2: } 7200 + 75\\text{ s}$$\n     $$\\text{Probe 3: } 7200 + 150\\text{ s}$$\n     $$\\dots$$\n     $$\\text{Probe 9: } 7200 + (8 \\times 75)\\text{ s} = 7800\\text{ s}$$\n3. **Termination:**\n   * After the 9th probe is sent, the kernel waits for one final interval of `tcp_keepalive_intvl` (75 seconds) for an acknowledgment.\n   * Total probe phase duration:\n     $$9 \\times 75\\text{ s} = 675\\text{ s}$$\n4. **Total Time:**\n   $$7200\\text{ s} + (9 \\times 75\\text{ s}) = 7200\\text{ s} + 675\\text{ s} = \\mathbf{7425\\text{ s}}$$\n   $$\\text{Converted: } \\mathbf{2\\text{ hours, } 3\\text{ minutes, and } 45\\text{ seconds}}$$\n\n---\n\n### Practical Caveats / Nuances\n\n* **TCP Retransmission Timer Granularity & TCP Timer Jitter:** The kernel handles keepalive checks on timer wheels / softirq ticks, so the actual time may deviate by a fraction of a second to a few seconds depending on timer slack and tick resolution (`CONFIG_HZ`).\n* **Error Reported:** Once the timer expires, the kernel transitions the TCP state to `CLOSE`, frees resources, and any subsequent read/write call on the socket by the application returns an error (typically `ETIMEDOUT`).\n"}],"lost":[],"rankings":[{"by":"antigravity-gemini-pro-high","ranking":[{"label":"Response A","rank":1,"reason":"Provides the correct final figure (7,875 seconds) along with the correct parameters, default values, and flawlessly executes the arithmetic."},{"label":"Response B","rank":1,"reason":"Matches Response A in correctness (7,875 seconds) and provides an accurate breakdown of the timeline along with a helpful practical caveat."},{"label":"Response C","rank":3,"reason":"Identifies the correct parameters and formula but fails on basic arithmetic (claiming 7200 + 675 = 7425), resulting in an incorrect final answer."}]},{"by":"antigravity-gemini-flash-high","ranking":[{"label":"Response A","rank":1,"reason":"Correctly calculates the total time as 7,875 seconds, identifies the three governing parameters, and provides an exceptionally clear timeline of kernel timer behavior distinguishing the last probe dispatch from the final teardown."},{"label":"Response B","rank":1,"reason":"Accurately identifies all three sysctl parameters, calculates the correct total duration of 7,875 seconds, and provides a clear, concise step-by-step explanation along with practical context."},{"label":"Response C","rank":3,"reason":"Correctly identifies the three parameters and writes the proper formula, but commits an arithmetic error (evaluating 7200 + 675 as 7425), leading to an incorrect final duration."}]},{"by":"antigravity-gemini-flash-low","ranking":[{"label":"Response A","rank":1,"reason":"It gives the correct total time of 7,875 seconds and parameters, supported by an exact, highly detailed kernel-level trace of probe counters and timer firings."},{"label":"Response B","rank":2,"reason":"It gives the correct figure of 7,875 seconds and the correct parameters with sound arithmetic and clear reasoning, just slightly less detailed regarding kernel implementation than Response A."},{"label":"Response C","rank":3,"reason":"It suffers from an elementary arithmetic error, stating that 7,200 + 675 equals 7,425 seconds instead of 7,875 seconds, corrupting the final answer."}]}],"aggregate":[{"label":"Response A","averageRank":1,"votes":3},{"label":"Response B","averageRank":1.333,"votes":3},{"label":"Response C","averageRank":3,"votes":3}],"judge":{"model":"antigravity-gemini-flash-low","blind":true},"calls":7}}}

